; SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-or-later

; ============================================================================
; CellRuleHost.rgr — the seam a custom cell rule arrives through.
; ============================================================================
;
; `DataValidation` answers "would this cell take that text?" from data alone:
; a list of choices, a numeric range, a length. That is deliberate — the same
; object is read out of an .xlsx and written back into one, and a rule that
; needed an interpreter to answer would be a rule the file format cannot hold.
;
; But a real column often wants something no range can say:
;
;     an SKU must match SKU-<digits>
;     an end date must not be before the start date in the row beside it
;     a discount over 30% is only allowed for a customer marked wholesale
;
; So `kind = "script"` names a function instead of stating a bound, and THIS
; is where the naming is resolved. A pane with no host accepts everything a
; script rule covers, which is the right default in two places at once: a
; sheet read out of a file, where the script is not there, and a host that
; never wired one up.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Why an object rather than an import
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;
; The implementation that runs the script is `CellScripts`, and it pulls in
; ComponentEngine and the TypeScript parser. Most things that embed a grid
; want neither. Handing the pane a host it can call, instead of having the
; pane import the engine, is what keeps a spreadsheet that has no scripts
; from carrying an interpreter.
; ============================================================================

class CellRuleHost {
    ; Whatever the last refusal was about, for a host that wants to say more
    ; than "not allowed here".
    def errorText:string ""

    Constructor () {
    }

    ; Is there anything behind this name? A rule naming a function nobody
    ; registered is not an error — see the header — but a caller may want to
    ; know before relying on it.
    fn hasRule:boolean (name:string) {
        return false
    }

    ; The verdict. A host that cannot answer must return TRUE: refusing what
    ; somebody typed because our own machinery was not wired up would lose
    ; their work for a reason that has nothing to do with them.
    fn accepts:boolean (name:string text:string row:int col:int) {
        return true
    }

    ; Why the last `accepts` said no. Empty when it said yes, or when the
    ; script gave no reason.
    fn reason:string () {
        return errorText
    }
}
